# Response headers for Cloudflare Workers Static Assets (MCD-11, PRD §32).
#
# The CSP is written against what the build actually emits, not a template
# (PRD §32: "CSP aligned with real dependencies"). As of MCD-11 the output is:
#
#   - zero executable JavaScript — every <script> is application/ld+json, a
#     data block browsers do not execute
#   - no images, no web fonts, no external resource loads
#   - inline <style> blocks from Astro's scoped styles, plus linked stylesheets
#
# `style-src` therefore needs 'unsafe-inline'. Hashes would be stricter, but
# they change on every build and this file is static, so they would silently go
# stale and break styling. The exposure is limited: no user input reaches the
# page, and script execution is already denied.
#
# HSTS is enabled here at MCD-13, which is the point PRD §32 allows: after the
# deployment is verified, not before. A browser that sees this header refuses
# plaintext for a year and cannot be talked out of it, so turning it on early —
# while TLS or a subdomain might still be wrong — locks visitors out of a site
# nobody can fix from the server side.
#
# `includeSubDomains` covers every host under meowcoder.com, so it was only
# added after checking that each one already serves valid TLS: study (200),
# shouri (200), and www (301 to the apex). Any future subdomain inherits the
# same requirement — it must be HTTPS from the moment it exists.
#
# `preload` is deliberately absent. Preloading bakes the domain into browser
# binaries, and removal takes months, so it is a separate decision from the
# header itself and not one this task needs.
#
# `static.cloudflareinsights.com` is Cloudflare Web Analytics, which the zone
# injects itself — the build emits no reference to it. It is cookieless and
# collects no personal data, and it supplies the page-view and language signals
# PRD §33 asks for. The outbound-click and case-study-engagement signals in §33
# need custom events, which Web Analytics does not support; that gap is
# recorded in TASK-11 rather than solved by adding a heavier vendor.
#
# Without these two entries the injected beacon is blocked, which shows up as a
# console error and a CSP violation in Lighthouse's Best Practices audit.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; manifest-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Astro fingerprints these filenames, so they can never go stale.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
